            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Constructions and Tilings"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.492,
            0.113
          ],
          "content": "**Angle Bisection for a Design**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.212,
            0.484,
            0.237
          ],
          "content": "How do we construct this figure?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.563,
            0.12,
            0.784,
            0.285
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.649,
                0.289,
                0.709,
                0.305
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.5"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.398,
            0.496,
            0.438
          ],
          "content": "The supporting lines for this figure will look like this."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.557,
            0.325,
            0.785,
            0.495
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.517,
            0.606,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": "What is the angle between two adjacent lines?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.542,
            0.846,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "We need the angle between every pair of adjacent lines to be equal. Since $3 6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ is equally divided into 8 parts, every angle is $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.591,
            0.846,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "? How do we construct a $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ angle using only a ruler and a compass? We know how to construct a $9 0 ^ { \\circ }$ angle. If we can divide it into two equal parts, or bisect it, then we get a $4 5 ^ { \\circ }$ angle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.705,
            0.629,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "We will now develop a general method to bisect any angle. Consider an angle ∠XOY."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.651,
            0.661,
            0.836,
            0.771
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.813,
            0.63,
            0.871
          ],
          "content": "We can bisect it if we can draw two congruent triangles ΔOBC and ΔOAC as shown in the figure. Then $\\angle BOC = \\angle AOC$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.649,
            0.789,
            0.837,
            0.895
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
